1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,370
Listen. Practice. Speak. Practice makes you perfect.

2
00:00:12,370 --> 00:00:19,260
Hi everyone. Thanks for joining us. If you're here to improve your English for real life situations,

3
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:22,260
especially at work, you're in the right place.

4
00:00:22,260 --> 00:00:30,260
Absolutely. And today's episode is going to be extremely useful because we're focusing on a skill you need almost every day in the professional world.

5
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:37,260
That's right. Today, we're talking about how to write a professional email in business English.

6
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:46,260
This is such an important topic. Whether you're working in an office, freelancing, studying, or applying for jobs, email writing is essential.

7
00:00:46,260 --> 00:00:52,260
Exactly. And don't worry, we're going to make this simple, practical, and very easy to understand.

8
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:59,260
We'll walk you through real examples, common mistakes, and even practice conversations so you can feel confident.

9
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:00,920
It sounds great.

10
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:03,520
So before we begin, how are you feeling today?

11
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:04,520
I'm feeling great.

12
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:08,920
I actually wrote about 15 emails this morning, so I think I'm in the perfect mood for this

13
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:09,920
topic.

14
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:10,920
How about you?

15
00:01:10,920 --> 00:01:11,920
Same here.

16
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:16,160
Emails are such a big part of my day, and I know many learners struggle with them.

17
00:01:16,160 --> 00:01:17,160
That's true.

18
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:18,760
Okay, everyone, let's get started.

19
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:19,760
All right.

20
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:21,960
Let's begin with a basic question.

21
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,180
What is a professional email?

22
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:30,000
A professional email is a clear, polite, and well-structured message used in formal or

23
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,780
business situations.

24
00:01:31,780 --> 00:01:34,820
So it's different from texting a friend, right?

25
00:01:34,820 --> 00:01:35,820
Exactly.

26
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:38,740
When you text a friend, you might say, hey, what's up?

27
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:42,860
But in a professional email, you need to be more respectful and structured.

28
00:01:42,860 --> 00:01:44,080
That makes sense.

29
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,900
So what's the first part of a professional email?

30
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:49,500
The first part is the subject line.

31
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,500
Many people ignore this, but it's very important.

32
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:56,520
Yes, because the subject line tells the reader what your email is about.

33
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:59,240
So it should be clear and specific, right?

34
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:00,240
Exactly.

35
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:04,800
For example, instead of writing, hello, you should write something like meeting request

36
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:07,400
for Friday or project update.

37
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:08,400
That's much better.

38
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,280
It saves time for the reader.

39
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:11,280
Right.

40
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:13,920
Now let's move to the next part, the greeting.

41
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:14,920
Okay.

42
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:16,840
This is how we start the email.

43
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:17,840
Yes.

44
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:24,000
And professional greetings include Dear Mr. Smith, Dear Ms. Brown, or Dear Team.

45
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:26,400
What if I don't know the person's name?

46
00:02:26,400 --> 00:02:31,160
You can say Dear Sir or Madam, but try to find the name if possible.

47
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:32,560
It feels more personal.

48
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:33,760
Can I say Hi?

49
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:38,540
Yes, but only if the relationship is more casual, or you already know the person.

50
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:42,200
For example, Hi John is fine in many workplaces.

51
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:43,200
Got it.

52
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,720
So we should always think about the situation.

53
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:46,880
Exactly.

54
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:50,100
Now after the greeting, we have the opening line.

55
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:52,560
This is where many learners feel confused.

56
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:53,740
What should they say?

57
00:02:53,740 --> 00:02:55,020
Keep it simple.

58
00:02:55,020 --> 00:02:58,980
You can say, I hope you are doing well, or thank you for your email.

59
00:02:58,980 --> 00:03:00,820
Those sound natural and polite.

60
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:01,820
Yes.

61
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:04,420
Now let's move to the main part of the email.

62
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:07,220
This is where you explain why you're writing.

63
00:03:07,220 --> 00:03:08,220
Exactly.

64
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:09,580
And this is very important.

65
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:11,740
You should be clear and direct.

66
00:03:11,740 --> 00:03:12,940
Can you give an example?

67
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:13,940
Sure.

68
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:19,460
Say, I am writing to request a meeting, or I am writing to inform you about the project

69
00:03:19,460 --> 00:03:20,460
update.

70
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:21,460
That's very clear.

71
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:22,460
I like that structure.

72
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:27,140
Yes, using I am writing to is very common in business English.

73
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:30,140
Now let's talk about a real-life example.

74
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,140
Imagine you want to request a meeting.

75
00:03:32,140 --> 00:03:33,140
Okay.

76
00:03:33,140 --> 00:03:35,900
Let's build the email step by step.

77
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:38,940
First the subject line, meeting request.

78
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:41,780
Then the greeting, Dear Mr. Johnson.

79
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:44,340
Opening, I hope you are doing well.

80
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:48,960
Main purpose, I am writing to request a meeting to discuss the new project.

81
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:53,340
And then add details, are you available on Monday or Tuesday?

82
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:55,300
And finally, we close politely.

83
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:57,540
I look forward to your response.

84
00:03:57,540 --> 00:03:59,980
That's a complete professional email.

85
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:01,100
Exactly.

86
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,260
Now let's talk about tone.

87
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:05,580
Tone is very important in emails.

88
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:10,660
Yes, you should always be polite and respectful, even if you're asking for something or

89
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:11,660
complaining.

90
00:04:11,660 --> 00:04:14,100
Let's talk about a difficult situation.

91
00:04:14,100 --> 00:04:15,820
What if you need to complain?

92
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:16,820
Good question.

93
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:18,860
You should never sound angry.

94
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:21,260
Instead, be calm and professional.

95
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:22,540
Can you give an example?

96
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:25,260
Instead of saying, this is unacceptable,

97
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:29,180
you can say, I would like to bring to your attention an issue.

98
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:30,700
That sounds much better.

99
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:31,340
Yes.

100
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:34,500
For example, I noticed that the delivery was delayed,

101
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,340
and I would appreciate your assistance

102
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:38,020
in resolving this issue.

103
00:04:38,020 --> 00:04:39,020
Very polite.

104
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:40,300
Exactly.

105
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:44,660
Now let's talk about another situation, following up on an email.

106
00:04:44,660 --> 00:04:47,100
This happens a lot when people don't reply.

107
00:04:47,100 --> 00:04:48,100
Yes.

108
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:51,740
You can say, I just wanted to follow up on my previous email.

109
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:53,340
That's simple and polite.

110
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:56,580
Or I would like to kindly remind you about...

111
00:04:56,580 --> 00:04:58,580
That sounds very professional.

112
00:04:58,580 --> 00:05:01,020
Now let's talk about closing the email.

113
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:03,640
Yes, this is the final impression.

114
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:08,980
Common closings include best regards, kind regards, or sincerely.

115
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:09,980
And then your name.

116
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:14,740
Exactly. You can also include your job title and contact information.

117
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:18,420
Now let's do a full example conversation using emails.

118
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:22,660
Great idea. I'll start. Imagine I'm writing to my manager.

119
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:23,820
Okay, go ahead.

120
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:26,500
Subject, Request for leave.

121
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:29,580
Dear Ms. Brown, I hope you are doing well.

122
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:34,420
I'm writing to request leave for two days next week due to personal reasons.

123
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:36,540
Please let me know if this is possible.

124
00:05:36,540 --> 00:05:38,420
Best regards, Sarah.

125
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:41,760
That's excellent. Clear, polite, and professional.

126
00:05:41,760 --> 00:05:42,760
Now your turn.

127
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:45,560
Subject, re-request for leave.

128
00:05:45,560 --> 00:05:48,160
Dear Sarah, thank you for your email.

129
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:50,160
Your leave request has been approved.

130
00:05:50,160 --> 00:05:53,220
Please make sure to complete your tasks before you leave.

131
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:54,860
Best regards, Daniel.

132
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:57,100
Perfect, that's a great response.

133
00:05:57,100 --> 00:05:59,660
Now let's talk about common mistakes.

134
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:01,800
Yes, this is very important.

135
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:05,100
Mistake number one, being too informal.

136
00:06:05,100 --> 00:06:08,460
like writing hey or what's up in a business email.

137
00:06:08,460 --> 00:06:12,020
Exactly. Avoid that in professional situations.

138
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:16,220
Mistake number two, writing long and confusing sentences.

139
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:19,000
Yes, keep your sentences short and clear.

140
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,860
Mistake number three, forgetting to check grammar and spelling.

141
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:24,820
This is very important.

142
00:06:24,820 --> 00:06:27,220
Always proofread your email before sending.

143
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:31,100
Mistake number four, not being clear about your purpose.

144
00:06:31,100 --> 00:06:34,660
Right. The reader should understand your message immediately.

145
00:06:34,660 --> 00:06:36,580
Now let's do a role play.

146
00:06:36,580 --> 00:06:37,500
Great idea.

147
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:39,940
Let's imagine you are writing to a client.

148
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:40,700
Okay.

149
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:41,980
Start your email.

150
00:06:41,980 --> 00:06:44,420
Subject, Project Update.

151
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:47,460
Dear Mr. Lee, I hope you are doing well.

152
00:06:47,460 --> 00:06:50,540
I'm writing to provide an update on the project.

153
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,500
We have completed the first phase

154
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,060
and are currently working on the second phase.

155
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:57,260
Please let me know if you have any questions.

156
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:59,060
Best regards, Sarah.

157
00:06:59,060 --> 00:06:59,900
Excellent.

158
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:01,820
Very clear and professional.

159
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:02,660
Thank you.

160
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:03,700
Now your turn.

161
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:10,500
Subject. Re-Project Update. Dear Sarah, thank you for the update. The progress sounds great.

162
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:14,420
Please keep me informed about the next steps. Best regards, Daniel.

163
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:18,020
Perfect. See how simple and clear these emails are?

164
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:22,820
Yes, that's the key. Keep it simple, polite, and structured.

165
00:07:22,820 --> 00:07:28,020
Exactly. Now let's summarize. First, write a clear subject line.

166
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:33,060
Second, use a proper greeting. Third, use a polite opening line.

167
00:07:33,060 --> 00:07:35,900
Fourth, clearly explain your purpose.

168
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:37,940
Fifth, close politely.

169
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:41,300
And finally, check your email before sending.

170
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:42,460
Great summary.

171
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:43,780
Now it's your turn.

172
00:07:43,780 --> 00:07:46,380
Try writing a professional email today.

173
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:48,780
Practice is the best way to improve.

174
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:51,060
Don't be afraid to make mistakes.

175
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:53,980
Yes, every email you write makes you better.

176
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:55,660
Thank you so much for listening.

177
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:57,180
Take care, keep practicing,

178
00:07:57,180 --> 00:07:59,180
and we'll see you in the next episode.

179
00:07:59,180 --> 00:08:00,100
Bye, everyone.

180
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:00,980
Goodbye.

181
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:06,660
Hello! Welcome back to Business English Talk. We're really happy to have you with us today.

182
00:08:06,660 --> 00:08:12,580
Hi everyone! Thanks for joining us. If you're here to improve your English, you're in the right place.

183
00:08:12,580 --> 00:08:19,380
I'm Emily. And I'm Jack. On this channel, we help you speak clear, natural English with confidence

184
00:08:19,380 --> 00:08:24,580
in real-life situations. If you enjoyed this lesson, please give us a thumbs up,

185
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:30,420
and don't forget to subscribe. Yes, your support helps us so much. Thank you for being here.

186
00:08:30,980 --> 00:08:33,940
So, Emily, what are we talking about today?

187
00:08:33,940 --> 00:08:37,460
Today we have a very practical and powerful topic.

188
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:38,900
We're going to learn how to write

189
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:41,980
a professional business email in English.

190
00:08:41,980 --> 00:08:43,860
That sounds really useful.

191
00:08:43,860 --> 00:08:46,900
I think many people struggle with writing emails.

192
00:08:46,900 --> 00:08:48,100
Absolutely.

193
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:49,860
Writing emails professionally

194
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:52,780
is a key skill in business communication.

195
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:54,500
So where do we start?

196
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:58,140
We start with understanding the purpose of the email.

197
00:08:58,140 --> 00:08:59,500
Before writing anything,

198
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:01,780
you should know why you are writing.

199
00:09:01,780 --> 00:09:03,820
So I should ask myself,

200
00:09:03,820 --> 00:09:06,020
what do I want from this email?

201
00:09:06,020 --> 00:09:07,100
Exactly.

202
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:08,980
Do you want to request something,

203
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:11,580
give information, or ask a question?

204
00:09:11,580 --> 00:09:12,860
That makes sense.

205
00:09:12,860 --> 00:09:14,500
If I don't know my purpose,

206
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:16,460
my email will not be clear.

207
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:18,740
Right, clarity is everything

208
00:09:18,740 --> 00:09:20,540
in business communication.

209
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:22,940
Okay, let's talk about structure.

210
00:09:22,940 --> 00:09:26,740
Yes, a professional email has five main parts,

211
00:09:26,740 --> 00:09:32,240
subject line, greeting, opening, main message, and closing.

212
00:09:32,240 --> 00:09:34,340
Let's start with the subject line.

213
00:09:34,340 --> 00:09:38,240
The subject line tells the reader what your email is about.

214
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:40,240
It should be short and clear.

215
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:41,840
Can you give examples?

216
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:46,640
Sure. Meeting request, job application, or project update.

217
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:50,440
So we should avoid unclear subjects like hello.

218
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:52,840
Exactly. Be specific.

219
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:54,140
Now the greeting.

220
00:09:54,140 --> 00:09:58,620
Yes, you can say Dear Mr. Smith or Dear Ms. Lee.

221
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:00,620
What if I don't know the name?

222
00:10:00,620 --> 00:10:05,180
You can say Dear Sir or Madam or Dear Hiring Manager.

223
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:06,700
Can I say Hi?

224
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:10,700
Only if you know the person well. Otherwise, stay formal.

225
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:13,420
Got it. Now the opening sentence.

226
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:16,860
The opening should clearly explain why you were writing.

227
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:17,900
Like what?

228
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:21,820
I hope you were doing well. I am writing to inquire about…

229
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:23,660
That sounds very natural.

230
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:26,240
Yes, and very professional.

231
00:10:26,240 --> 00:10:28,220
What about the main message?

232
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:30,280
This is where you give details.

233
00:10:30,280 --> 00:10:32,560
Keep it clear and organized.

234
00:10:32,560 --> 00:10:34,560
Should we write long paragraphs?

235
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:35,560
No.

236
00:10:35,560 --> 00:10:38,320
Use short sentences and simple words.

237
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:39,800
Can you give an example?

238
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:40,800
Sure.

239
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:42,400
I would like to schedule a meeting.

240
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:45,160
I am available on Monday or Tuesday.

241
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:47,160
That's easy to understand.

242
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:48,440
Exactly.

243
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:49,440
Simple is better.

244
00:10:49,440 --> 00:10:52,340
Now, let's talk about tone.

245
00:10:52,340 --> 00:10:54,780
One means how your message sounds.

246
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:58,420
In business emails, it should be polite and respectful.

247
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:02,460
So instead of saying, send me the file, I should say,

248
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:04,860
Could you please send me the file?

249
00:11:04,860 --> 00:11:06,460
That sounds much better.

250
00:11:06,460 --> 00:11:07,460
Yes.

251
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:12,500
Always use polite phrases like please, could you, and I would appreciate.

252
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:14,220
Now the closing part.

253
00:11:14,220 --> 00:11:16,660
You should end your email politely.

254
00:11:16,660 --> 00:11:18,060
What can we say?

255
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:21,740
Thank you for your time, or I look forward to your reply.

256
00:11:21,740 --> 00:11:22,740
And then?

257
00:11:22,740 --> 00:11:26,660
Use best regards or kind regards and your name.

258
00:11:26,660 --> 00:11:29,460
Let's put everything together in one example.

259
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:30,460
Great idea.

260
00:11:30,460 --> 00:11:32,580
Subject, meeting request.

261
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:35,780
Dear Mr. Lee, I hope you are doing well.

262
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:38,100
I am writing to request a meeting.

263
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:40,980
I would like to discuss the project timeline.

264
00:11:40,980 --> 00:11:43,900
I am available on Wednesday or Thursday.

265
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:46,220
Please let me know what works for you.

266
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:47,860
Thank you for your time.

267
00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:49,620
Best regards, Jack.

268
00:11:49,620 --> 00:11:51,020
That's a perfect email.

269
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:53,420
Yes, simple and professional.

270
00:11:53,420 --> 00:11:56,180
Now, let's talk about common mistakes.

271
00:11:56,180 --> 00:11:57,300
Good idea.

272
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:58,460
First mistake?

273
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:00,500
Being too informal.

274
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:03,100
Like using hey or emojis?

275
00:12:03,100 --> 00:12:06,300
Exactly. Avoid those in business emails.

276
00:12:06,300 --> 00:12:07,580
Second mistake?

277
00:12:07,580 --> 00:12:10,540
Writing long and confusing sentences.

278
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:12,180
So, keep it short.

279
00:12:12,180 --> 00:12:13,060
Yes.

280
00:12:13,060 --> 00:12:14,300
Third mistake?

281
00:12:14,300 --> 00:12:16,820
Not checking grammar and spelling.

282
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:19,220
That can look very unprofessional.

283
00:12:19,220 --> 00:12:21,480
Always proofread your email.

284
00:12:21,480 --> 00:12:23,080
Let's do a role play.

285
00:12:23,080 --> 00:12:24,020
Great.

286
00:12:24,020 --> 00:12:27,680
Dear hiring manager, I am applying for the position.

287
00:12:27,680 --> 00:12:30,320
Good start. Add your experience.

288
00:12:30,320 --> 00:12:33,220
I have two years of experience in marketing.

289
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:35,520
Nice. Now close the email.

290
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:38,920
Thank you for your time. I look forward to your reply.

291
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:39,920
Perfect.

292
00:12:39,920 --> 00:12:41,320
Let's try another one.

293
00:12:41,320 --> 00:12:42,220
Okay.

294
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:44,620
Hi. I want information.

295
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:46,420
That's not professional.

296
00:12:46,420 --> 00:12:47,820
Let me fix it.

297
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:52,240
Dear sir or madam, I would like to request more information.

298
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:53,340
Much better.

299
00:12:53,340 --> 00:12:55,740
Could you please send me the details?

300
00:12:55,740 --> 00:12:56,780
Excellent.

301
00:12:56,780 --> 00:12:59,780
Now let's talk about different situations.

302
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:03,580
Yes, like requests, follow-ups, and complaints.

303
00:13:03,580 --> 00:13:05,280
Example of request?

304
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:07,420
Could you please send the report?

305
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:08,380
Follow-up.

306
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:11,520
I am writing to follow-up on my previous email.

307
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:12,520
Complaint.

308
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:15,480
I am writing to express my concern about...

309
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:17,320
These are very useful.

310
00:13:17,320 --> 00:13:20,340
Yes, you can use them in many situations.

311
00:13:20,340 --> 00:13:21,560
Final tips?

312
00:13:21,560 --> 00:13:24,640
Keep your email clear, polite, and simple.

313
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:26,840
Don't use complicated words.

314
00:13:26,840 --> 00:13:29,880
Exactly. Simple English is powerful.

315
00:13:29,880 --> 00:13:31,480
Practice regularly.

316
00:13:31,480 --> 00:13:33,880
Yes, practice makes you better.

317
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:35,520
That was a great lesson.

318
00:13:35,520 --> 00:13:36,520
I agree.

