just real examples and conversations.

335
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:52,160
Yes, and we'll walk through everything step by step in a natural conversation, so by the

336
00:14:52,160 --> 00:14:56,560
end of this episode, you'll feel much more confident writing emails.

337
00:14:56,560 --> 00:14:57,560
Sounds perfect.

338
00:14:57,560 --> 00:15:00,480
So Emma, before we begin, how are you feeling today?

339
00:15:00,480 --> 00:15:01,480
I'm feeling great!

340
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:06,040
I've already written several emails this morning, so I'm definitely in the email

341
00:15:06,040 --> 00:15:07,040
mindset.

342
00:15:07,040 --> 00:15:08,040
How about you?

343
00:15:08,040 --> 00:15:09,040
Same here.

344
00:15:09,040 --> 00:15:11,400
Emails are basically part of my daily routine.

345
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:14,680
I think this lesson will be very useful for everyone listening.

346
00:15:14,680 --> 00:15:15,680
I agree.

347
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:18,080
Okay, everyone, let's get started.

348
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:21,360
Alright, let's begin with a simple question.

349
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:25,040
Why is writing a professional email so important?

350
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:26,240
Good question.

351
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:30,480
Because your email is often the first impression you make in a business situation.

352
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:31,600
Exactly.

353
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:35,640
Before someone meets you or hears your voice, they read your email.

354
00:15:35,640 --> 00:15:41,120
Yes, and if your email is unclear, rude, or unprofessional, it can create a bad

355
00:15:41,120 --> 00:15:42,720
even if you didn't mean it.

356
00:15:42,720 --> 00:15:43,720
That's true.

357
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:49,520
On the other hand, a well-written email shows that you are organized, respectful, and professional.

358
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:52,520
So basically, your email represents you.

359
00:15:52,520 --> 00:15:53,720
Perfectly said.

360
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:57,120
Now let's talk about the structure of a professional email.

361
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:59,320
Yes, this is very important.

362
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:03,120
Every professional email usually has five main parts.

363
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:05,520
Okay, let's go through them one by one.

364
00:16:05,520 --> 00:16:07,320
First, the subject line.

365
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:08,720
Second, the greeting.

366
00:16:08,720 --> 00:16:10,920
Third, the opening sentence.

367
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:13,060
Fourth, the main message.

368
00:16:13,060 --> 00:16:15,580
And fifth, the closing and sign off.

369
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:16,580
Great!

370
00:16:16,580 --> 00:16:17,780
Let's start with the subject line.

371
00:16:17,780 --> 00:16:21,620
Alright, the subject line is the first thing people see.

372
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:24,260
It tells the reader what your email is about.

373
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:27,320
Exactly, and it should be clear and specific.

374
00:16:27,320 --> 00:16:32,780
For example, instead of writing hello or question, you should write something like

375
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:37,060
meeting request for Monday or update on project deadline.

376
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:40,500
Yes, vague subject lines are a common mistake.

377
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:42,480
Let's give some practical examples.

378
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:45,720
Okay, bad example, important.

379
00:16:45,720 --> 00:16:47,560
Good example, important.

380
00:16:47,560 --> 00:16:49,480
Budget approval needed by Friday.

381
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:50,480
Nice.

382
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:52,760
Another one, bad, high.

383
00:16:52,760 --> 00:16:53,760
Good.

384
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:56,880
Follow up on job application, marketing assistant role.

385
00:16:56,880 --> 00:16:57,880
Perfect.

386
00:16:57,880 --> 00:16:59,640
So, the key is clarity.

387
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:00,640
Exactly.

388
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:03,840
Make it easy for the reader to understand your purpose immediately.

389
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:06,640
Alright, let's move on to the greeting.

390
00:17:06,640 --> 00:17:08,560
This is how you start your email.

391
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:12,280
Yes, and it depends on how formal the situation is.

392
00:17:12,280 --> 00:17:17,460
If it's very formal, you can say Dear Mr. Smith or Dear Ms. Smith.

393
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:21,140
If it's slightly less formal, you can say Dear John.

394
00:17:21,140 --> 00:17:26,080
And if it's more relaxed, especially with colleagues, you can say Hi John or Hello

395
00:17:26,080 --> 00:17:27,080
Sarah.

396
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:28,080
Exactly.

397
00:17:28,080 --> 00:17:29,080
But be careful.

398
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:34,420
In business situations, it's always safer to be slightly more formal than too casual.

399
00:17:34,420 --> 00:17:35,460
Good point.

400
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:38,080
What about if you don't know the person's name?

401
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:42,840
Then you can say, Dear Sir or Madam, or Dear Hiring Manager.

402
00:17:42,840 --> 00:17:43,840
Perfect.

403
00:17:43,840 --> 00:17:46,560
Now let's talk about the opening sentence.

404
00:17:46,560 --> 00:17:48,960
This is where many people make mistakes.

405
00:17:48,960 --> 00:17:53,680
Yes, some people go straight to the request without any introduction, which can sound

406
00:17:53,680 --> 00:17:54,680
rude.

407
00:17:54,680 --> 00:17:55,680
Exactly.

408
00:17:55,680 --> 00:17:58,120
It's better to start with a polite opening.

409
00:17:58,120 --> 00:18:03,480
For example, I hope you are doing well, or I hope this email finds you well, or

410
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:05,520
thank you for your previous email.

411
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:09,040
Yes, these phrases create a polite and friendly tone.

412
00:18:09,040 --> 00:18:10,840
Let's give a real example.

413
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:13,520
Okay, imagine you're emailing your manager.

414
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:16,560
You could say, hi, Sarah, I hope you're doing well.

415
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:19,080
I'm writing to update you on the project.

416
00:18:19,080 --> 00:18:21,400
That sounds natural and professional.

417
00:18:21,400 --> 00:18:23,840
Now, let's move to the main message.

418
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:25,600
This is the most important part.

419
00:18:25,600 --> 00:18:28,760
Yes, here you explain why you are writing.

420
00:18:28,760 --> 00:18:32,160
And the key rule is be clear and concise.

421
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:33,160
Absolutely.

422
00:18:33,160 --> 00:18:36,200
Don't write long, confusing sentences.

423
00:18:36,200 --> 00:18:38,800
Break your message into short paragraphs.

424
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:40,000
Let's give an example.

425
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:41,000
Alright?

426
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,120
Imagine you want to request a meeting.

427
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:48,240
You could write, I would like to schedule a meeting to discuss the new marketing strategy.

428
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:51,760
Are you available on Tuesday or Wednesday afternoon?

429
00:18:51,760 --> 00:18:53,360
That's clear and polite.

430
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:54,360
Yes.

431
00:18:54,360 --> 00:18:56,640
Now let's compare it with a bad example.

432
00:18:56,640 --> 00:18:57,640
Okay.

433
00:18:57,640 --> 00:19:01,520
I want meetings soon because we need to talk about many things.

434
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:04,200
That sounds unclear and a bit rude.

435
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:05,200
Exactly.

436
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,760
It's not specific and not professional.

437
00:19:07,760 --> 00:19:09,480
Now let's talk about tone.

438
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:11,840
Ah, this is very important.

439
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:15,800
Yes, your tone should always be polite and respectful.

440
00:19:15,800 --> 00:19:19,680
Instead of saying, send me the report, you should say, could you please send me the

441
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:20,680
report?

442
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:23,880
Or, I would appreciate it if you could send the report.

443
00:19:23,880 --> 00:19:25,320
That sounds much better.

444
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:27,560
Now let's talk about making requests.

445
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:30,900
Yes, this is something people do a lot in emails.

446
00:19:30,900 --> 00:19:33,780
There are many polite ways to make requests.

447
00:19:33,780 --> 00:19:35,960
For example, could you please?

448
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:37,540
Or would you mind?

449
00:19:37,540 --> 00:19:39,740
Or I was wondering if you could?

450
00:19:39,740 --> 00:19:42,300
These phrases soften your request.

451
00:19:42,300 --> 00:19:43,900
Let's give a real scenario.

452
00:19:43,900 --> 00:19:46,820
Okay, you need information from a colleague.

453
00:19:46,820 --> 00:19:50,700
You can say, could you please provide the updated figures by tomorrow?

454
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:51,700
Perfect.

455
00:19:51,700 --> 00:19:54,380
Now, let's talk about replying to emails.

456
00:19:54,380 --> 00:19:56,740
Yes, this is also important.

457
00:19:56,740 --> 00:20:00,660
When replying, you should always acknowledge the previous message.

458
00:20:00,660 --> 00:20:05,280
For example, thank you for your email, or thanks for getting back to me.

459
00:20:05,280 --> 00:20:08,520
Then you answer the question or provide the information.

460
00:20:08,520 --> 00:20:10,280
Let's do a quick role play.

461
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:11,280
Great idea.

462
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:12,280
Hi, Emma.

463
00:20:12,280 --> 00:20:14,120
Could you send me the latest sales report?

464
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:15,320
Hi, Janiel.

465
00:20:15,320 --> 00:20:16,320
Thanks for your email.

466
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:18,480
I've attached the latest sales report.

467
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:20,560
Please let me know if you need anything else.

468
00:20:20,560 --> 00:20:21,560
That's perfect.

469
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:23,600
Simple, clear, and polite.

470
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:26,040
Now let's talk about closing your email.

471
00:20:26,040 --> 00:20:28,960
Yes, this is how you finish your message.

472
00:20:28,960 --> 00:20:33,700
You can say, thank you for your time, or I look forward to your reply.

473
00:20:33,700 --> 00:20:36,640
Or please let me know if you have any questions.

474
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:39,140
These are very common and professional.

475
00:20:39,140 --> 00:20:40,640
Then comes the sign-off.

476
00:20:40,640 --> 00:20:43,560
Right, this is the final line.

477
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:47,260
Formal options include kind regards, best regards.

478
00:20:47,260 --> 00:20:51,140
Less formal options include best or thanks.

479
00:20:51,140 --> 00:20:52,300
Then your name.

480
00:20:52,300 --> 00:20:53,300
Exactly.

481
00:20:53,300 --> 00:20:55,460
Now let's talk about common mistakes.

482
00:20:55,460 --> 00:20:57,940
Oh yes, this is important.

483
00:20:57,940 --> 00:21:00,580
Mistake number one, being too informal.

484
00:21:00,580 --> 00:21:04,300
For example, writing, hey dude in a business email.

485
00:21:04,300 --> 00:21:06,540
That's definitely not appropriate.

486
00:21:06,540 --> 00:21:09,140
Mistake number two, using poor grammar.

487
00:21:09,140 --> 00:21:13,220
Yes, always check your spelling and grammar before sending.

488
00:21:13,220 --> 00:21:16,920
Mistake number three, writing very long emails.

489
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:17,920
People are busy.

490
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:20,100
Keep your message short and clear.

491
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:23,100
Mistake number four, forgetting the subject line.

492
00:21:23,100 --> 00:21:24,420
That's a big one.

493
00:21:24,420 --> 00:21:26,980
Mistake number five, being too direct.

494
00:21:26,980 --> 00:21:29,500
Yes, always use polite language.

495
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:32,100
Now let's do a full example together.

496
00:21:32,100 --> 00:21:33,140
Great idea.

497
00:21:33,140 --> 00:21:37,300
Subject line, application for marketing assistant position.

498
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:39,700
Greeting, dear hiring manager.

499
00:21:39,700 --> 00:21:41,980
Opening, I hope you were doing well.

500
00:21:41,980 --> 00:21:44,180
Main message, I'm writing to apply

501
00:21:44,180 --> 00:21:46,180
for the marketing assistant position.

502
00:21:46,180 --> 00:21:47,660
I have relevant experience

503
00:21:47,660 --> 00:21:49,700
and I'm very interested in this role.

504
00:21:49,700 --> 00:21:52,780
Add, please find my CV attached.

505
00:21:52,780 --> 00:21:54,360
I would welcome the opportunity

506
00:21:54,360 --> 00:21:56,540
to discuss my application further.

507
00:21:56,540 --> 00:21:59,660
Closing, thank you for your time and consideration.

508
00:21:59,660 --> 00:22:02,380
Sign off, kind regards, Emma.

509
00:22:02,380 --> 00:22:04,500
That's a perfect professional email.

510
00:22:04,500 --> 00:22:06,780
Yes, simple but effective.

511
00:22:06,780 --> 00:22:09,220
Now let's do one final role play.

512
00:22:09,220 --> 00:22:10,060
Great.

513
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:12,300
Hi Daniel, I hope you're doing well.

514
00:22:12,300 --> 00:22:13,940
I'm writing to ask if you could review

515
00:22:13,940 --> 00:22:15,420
the attached proposal.

516
00:22:15,420 --> 00:22:17,580
Hi Emma, thanks for your email.

517
00:22:17,580 --> 00:22:21,020
I've received the proposal and I'll review it by tomorrow.

518
00:22:21,020 --> 00:22:22,260
Great, thank you.

519
00:22:22,260 --> 00:22:24,300
Please let me know if you have any feedback.

520
00:22:24,300 --> 00:22:26,340
Sure, I'll get back to you soon.

521
00:22:26,340 --> 00:22:31,620
Perfect. That's a natural professional exchange. Yes, and very practical.

