534
00:23:36,080 --> 00:23:41,240
That's right. Today we're going to guide you step by step with real examples and natural

535
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:42,240
conversations.

536
00:23:42,240 --> 00:23:47,800
So, let's begin. First question, what is the first thing you write in an email?

537
00:23:47,800 --> 00:23:50,560
The greeting. Always start with a greeting.

538
00:23:50,560 --> 00:23:55,000
Yes. And choosing the right greeting is very important because it creates the

539
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:56,400
first impression.

540
00:23:56,400 --> 00:24:01,680
For example, if you are writing to someone formally, you can say, Dear Mr. Smith.

541
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:04,080
Or Dear Ms. Johnson.

542
00:24:04,080 --> 00:24:07,800
But if the situation is less formal, you can say, Hi, John.

543
00:24:07,800 --> 00:24:09,440
Or Hello, Sarah.

544
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:10,440
Exactly.

545
00:24:10,440 --> 00:24:13,680
But avoid using, Hey, in professional emails.

546
00:24:13,680 --> 00:24:14,680
It's too casual.

547
00:24:14,680 --> 00:24:16,840
Yes, that's a common mistake.

548
00:24:16,840 --> 00:24:20,160
Okay, now after the greeting, what comes next?

549
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:21,640
The opening sentence.

550
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:22,640
Right.

551
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:25,200
This is where you explain why you are writing.

552
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:28,520
A very common phrase is, I hope you're doing well.

553
00:24:28,520 --> 00:24:30,720
Yes, that's polite and friendly.

554
00:24:30,720 --> 00:24:34,720
Another example is, I am writing to follow up on our meeting.

555
00:24:34,720 --> 00:24:37,680
Or I am writing regarding your request.

556
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:41,240
The key is to be clear and direct, but still polite.

557
00:24:41,240 --> 00:24:44,560
Exactly. Don't make the reader guess your purpose.

558
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:46,200
Let's give a real example.

559
00:24:46,200 --> 00:24:47,000
Okay.

560
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,200
Hi, Sarah. I hope you're doing well.

561
00:24:49,200 --> 00:24:51,720
I am writing to update you on the project.

562
00:24:51,720 --> 00:24:54,280
Perfect. That's clear and professional.

563
00:24:54,280 --> 00:24:57,720
Now let's move to the main part of the email, the body.

564
00:24:57,720 --> 00:25:00,360
Yes, this is where you give details.

565
00:25:00,360 --> 00:25:03,880
And it's important to keep your sentences short and clear.

566
00:25:03,880 --> 00:25:06,920
Don't write long, complicated paragraphs.

567
00:25:06,920 --> 00:25:09,400
Exactly. Use simple English.

568
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:13,800
Let's talk about making requests. This is very common in emails.

569
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:17,880
Yes, for example, you can say, could you please send me the report?

570
00:25:17,880 --> 00:25:19,640
That's polite and clear.

571
00:25:19,640 --> 00:25:22,200
If you want to be more formal, you can say,

572
00:25:22,200 --> 00:25:25,320
I would appreciate it if you could send me the report.

573
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:27,400
That sounds very professional.

574
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:29,080
Now let's compare something.

575
00:25:29,080 --> 00:25:29,960
Okay.

576
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:31,400
Send me the report.

577
00:25:31,400 --> 00:25:33,000
That sounds rude.

578
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:33,800
Yes.

579
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:36,440
But could you please send me the report?

580
00:25:36,440 --> 00:25:37,720
Sounds polite.

581
00:25:37,720 --> 00:25:38,920
Big difference.

582
00:25:38,920 --> 00:25:41,640
Tone is everything in email writing.

583
00:25:41,640 --> 00:25:44,440
Let's give more examples of polite language.

584
00:25:44,440 --> 00:25:45,000
Sure.

585
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:48,520
Instead of saying you didn't reply, you can say,

586
00:25:48,520 --> 00:25:51,400
I just wanted to follow up on my previous email.

587
00:25:51,400 --> 00:25:52,920
That sounds much better.

588
00:25:52,920 --> 00:25:56,360
Instead of saying, I need this now, you can say,

589
00:25:56,360 --> 00:25:59,640
could you please send this at your earliest convenience?

590
00:25:59,640 --> 00:26:01,320
That's very professional.

591
00:26:01,320 --> 00:26:03,800
Now let's talk about giving information.

592
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:06,040
Yes, another common situation.

593
00:26:06,040 --> 00:26:09,160
You can say, I would like to inform you that…

594
00:26:09,160 --> 00:26:11,240
Or just to let you know.

595
00:26:11,240 --> 00:26:12,840
The first one is more formal.

596
00:26:12,840 --> 00:26:15,800
The second is more casual, but still professional.

597
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:17,320
Let's do an example.

598
00:26:17,320 --> 00:26:22,760
Hi team, I would like to inform you that the meeting has been rescheduled to 3pm.

599
00:26:22,760 --> 00:26:23,880
Very clear.

600
00:26:23,880 --> 00:26:26,440
Now let's move to closing the email.

601
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:29,000
Yes, how do we end professionally?

602
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:32,920
You can use phrases like please let me know if you have any questions.

603
00:26:32,920 --> 00:26:35,400
Or looking forward to your reply.

604
00:26:35,400 --> 00:26:37,400
Or thank you for your time.

605
00:26:37,400 --> 00:26:38,920
These are very useful.

606
00:26:38,920 --> 00:26:41,480
And after that you add a sign-off.

607
00:26:41,480 --> 00:26:43,400
Like best regards.

608
00:26:43,400 --> 00:26:45,080
Or kind regards.

609
00:26:45,080 --> 00:26:46,440
Or sincerely.

610
00:26:46,440 --> 00:26:48,760
Best regards is the safest option.

611
00:26:48,760 --> 00:26:51,440
Let's build a full email together.

612
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:52,760
Okay, let's do it.

613
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:54,880
Hi, John. I hope you're doing well.

614
00:26:54,880 --> 00:26:58,000
I'm writing to request the latest sales report.

615
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,280
Could you please send it to me by Friday?

616
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:03,240
Please let me know if you need any additional information.

617
00:27:03,240 --> 00:27:05,040
Best regards, Emily.

618
00:27:05,040 --> 00:27:08,160
Perfect. That's a complete professional email.

619
00:27:08,160 --> 00:27:11,000
Now let's talk about common mistakes.

620
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,480
Yes, this part is very important.

621
00:27:13,480 --> 00:27:16,560
Mistake number one, being too direct.

622
00:27:16,560 --> 00:27:19,040
Like saying, send me the file.

623
00:27:19,040 --> 00:27:22,040
Always add polite words like please.

624
00:27:22,040 --> 00:27:25,240
Mistake number two, using informal language.

625
00:27:25,240 --> 00:27:27,600
Like hey or what's up.

626
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:29,800
Avoid those in business emails.

627
00:27:29,800 --> 00:27:32,640
Mistake number three, grammar mistakes.

628
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:35,400
Always check your email before sending.

629
00:27:35,400 --> 00:27:38,480
Mistake number four, writing too much.

630
00:27:38,480 --> 00:27:40,800
Keep your message short and clear.

631
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:43,840
Mistake number five, no subject line.

632
00:27:43,840 --> 00:27:47,520
Always include a clear subject to like meeting update.

633
00:27:47,520 --> 00:27:49,920
Now let's practice with a role play.

634
00:27:49,920 --> 00:27:51,040
Great idea.

635
00:27:51,040 --> 00:27:53,360
Hi Alex, I hope you're doing well.

636
00:27:53,360 --> 00:27:56,240
I'm writing to ask about the client presentation.

637
00:27:56,240 --> 00:27:57,440
That sounds good.

638
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:00,640
Could you please share the latest version of the slides?

639
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:01,760
Very polite.

640
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:02,960
Thank you in advance.

641
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:04,800
Looking forward to your reply.

642
00:28:04,800 --> 00:28:05,680
Excellent.

643
00:28:05,680 --> 00:28:07,360
Let's do another example.

644
00:28:07,360 --> 00:28:08,720
Job application.

645
00:28:08,720 --> 00:28:09,520
Okay.

646
00:28:09,520 --> 00:28:12,720
Dear hiring manager, I hope you are doing well.

647
00:28:12,720 --> 00:28:15,440
I am writing to apply for the marketing position.

648
00:28:15,440 --> 00:28:17,280
Very professional opening.

649
00:28:17,280 --> 00:28:21,680
I have three years of experience and I am very interested in this role.

650
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:22,400
Good.

651
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:24,720
Please find my CV attached.

652
00:28:24,720 --> 00:28:26,560
Thank you for your consideration.

653
00:28:26,560 --> 00:28:27,920
Perfect closing.

654
00:28:27,920 --> 00:28:29,520
Now let's do one more.

655
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:30,720
Complete email.

656
00:28:30,720 --> 00:28:31,520
Okay.

657
00:28:31,520 --> 00:28:37,440
Dear sir or madam, I am writing to express my concern about a recent order.

658
00:28:37,440 --> 00:28:38,560
Very formal.

659
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:42,800
The product arrived damaged and I would like to request a replacement.

660
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:44,320
Clear and polite.

661
00:28:44,320 --> 00:28:47,520
I would appreciate your help in resolving this issue.

662
00:28:47,520 --> 00:28:48,880
Excellent tone.

663
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:51,120
Now let's summarize everything.

664
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:52,960
Start with a proper greeting.

665
00:28:52,960 --> 00:28:55,280
Write a clear opening sentence.

666
00:28:55,280 --> 00:28:57,280
Explain your message simply.

667
00:28:57,280 --> 00:28:59,040
Use polite language.

668
00:28:59,040 --> 00:29:00,960
End with a closing sentence.

669
00:29:00,960 --> 00:29:03,440
And finish with a professional sign-off.

670
00:29:03,440 --> 00:29:06,000
Keep it simple, clear, and polite.

671
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:07,680
Practice is the key.

672
00:29:07,680 --> 00:29:09,560
Don't be afraid to make mistakes.

673
00:29:09,560 --> 00:29:12,280
Every email you write helps you improve.

674
00:29:12,280 --> 00:29:14,480
So keep writing and keep learning.

675
00:29:14,480 --> 00:29:16,800
Thank you so much for listening today.

676
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:20,160
We hope this lesson helps you write better professional emails.

677
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:22,000
See you in the next episode.

678
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:23,740
Goodbye, everyone.

679
00:29:23,740 --> 00:29:27,060
Hello! Welcome Business English Learners!

680
00:29:27,060 --> 00:29:29,580
We're really happy to have you with us today.

681
00:29:29,580 --> 00:29:32,300
Hi everyone! Thanks for joining us.

682
00:29:32,300 --> 00:29:34,460
If you're here to improve your English,

683
00:29:34,460 --> 00:29:37,620
especially your professional communication skills,

684
00:29:37,620 --> 00:29:40,020
you're in exactly the right place.

685
00:29:40,020 --> 00:29:41,020
I'm Marco.

686
00:29:41,020 --> 00:29:42,540
And I'm Erika.

687
00:29:42,540 --> 00:29:47,300
And today we're focusing on something extremely practical and important.

688
00:29:47,300 --> 00:29:50,900
How to write a professional email in business English.

689
00:29:50,900 --> 00:29:54,700
Yes, this is a skill that can truly change your career.

690
00:29:54,700 --> 00:29:59,340
Whether you're applying for a job, communicating with your manager or writing to clients,

691
00:29:59,340 --> 00:30:01,100
email is everywhere.

692
00:30:01,100 --> 00:30:02,260
Absolutely.

693
00:30:02,260 --> 00:30:06,500
In today's professional world, your email is often your first impression.

694
00:30:06,500 --> 00:30:09,680
And as we all know, first impressions matter.

695
00:30:09,680 --> 00:30:10,780
Exactly.

696
00:30:10,780 --> 00:30:16,680
So today, we're going to help you write emails that are clear, polite, professional, and effective.

697
00:30:16,680 --> 00:30:23,260
We'll walk you through real examples, common mistakes, useful phrases, and even some role plays.

698
00:30:23,260 --> 00:30:24,700
Sounds perfect.

699
00:30:24,700 --> 00:30:27,340
So before we jump in, let me ask you something.

700
00:30:27,340 --> 00:30:29,540
How often do you write emails?

701
00:30:29,540 --> 00:30:31,900
Honestly, every single day.

702
00:30:31,900 --> 00:30:34,200
Emails are a big part of my work.

703
00:30:34,200 --> 00:30:38,160
I write to colleagues, clients, and sometimes new contacts.

704
00:30:38,160 --> 00:30:39,160
Same here.

705
00:30:39,160 --> 00:30:42,080
I think I write at least 10 emails today.

706
00:30:42,080 --> 00:30:45,400
Some are short, but some need to be very carefully written.

707
00:30:45,400 --> 00:30:46,400
That's true.

708
00:30:46,400 --> 00:30:48,520
Not all emails are the same.

709
00:30:48,520 --> 00:30:53,760
Some are quick and informal, but business emails need structure and professionalism.

710
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:54,760
Exactly.

711
00:30:54,760 --> 00:30:56,920
So let's start from the very beginning.

712
00:30:56,920 --> 00:30:58,920
What makes an email professional?

713
00:30:58,920 --> 00:31:04,360
A professional email is clear, polite, well-structured, and respectful.

714
00:31:04,360 --> 00:31:08,800
It avoids slang, uses correct grammar, and gets straight to the point.

715
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:10,080
I like that.

716
00:31:10,080 --> 00:31:11,640
Clear and respectful.

717
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:13,160
Those are key ideas.

718
00:31:13,160 --> 00:31:16,680
Yes, and also it should follow a proper format.

719
00:31:16,680 --> 00:31:19,760
Right, so let's break it down step by step.

720
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:23,040
What is the first thing we need in a professional email?

721
00:31:23,040 --> 00:31:24,040
The subject line.

722
00:31:24,040 --> 00:31:26,560
Ah, yes, the subject line.

723
00:31:26,560 --> 00:31:29,520
Many people underestimate it, but it's very important.

724
00:31:29,520 --> 00:31:32,920
The subject line tells the reader what the email is about.

725
00:31:32,920 --> 00:31:34,880
It should be short and clear.

726
00:31:34,880 --> 00:31:36,640
Can you give some examples?

727
00:31:36,640 --> 00:31:37,640
Sure.

728
00:31:37,640 --> 00:31:43,900
Meeting request for Monday, project update, or application for marketing position.

729
00:31:43,900 --> 00:31:46,560
Those are very clear, no confusion.

730
00:31:46,560 --> 00:31:50,120
Avoid vague subjects like hello or important.

731
00:31:50,120 --> 00:31:52,080
They don't give enough information.

732
00:31:52,080 --> 00:31:53,080
Got it.

733
00:31:53,080 --> 00:31:55,280
So clear and specific subject lines.

734
00:31:55,280 --> 00:31:56,280
Exactly.

735
00:31:56,280 --> 00:31:59,280
Now, after the subject line, what comes next?

736
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:00,280
The greeting.

737
00:32:00,280 --> 00:32:01,280
Right.

738
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:03,040
This is how you start the email.

739
00:32:03,040 --> 00:32:07,040
In business English, greetings are usually formal or semi-formal.

740
00:32:07,040 --> 00:32:13,440
If you know the person's name, you can say, Dear Mr. Smith or Dear Ms. Johnson.

741
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:15,360
And what if you don't know their name?

742
00:32:15,360 --> 00:32:18,440
Then you can say, Dear Sir or Madame.

743
00:32:18,440 --> 00:32:20,280
That sounds very formal.

744
00:32:20,280 --> 00:32:21,280
It is.

745
00:32:21,280 --> 00:32:24,120
Another option is to whom it may concern.

746
00:32:24,120 --> 00:32:26,200
What about Hi or Hello?

747
00:32:26,200 --> 00:32:31,200
Hi, John, or hello, Sarah, is okay if you already know the person.

748
00:32:31,200 --> 00:32:33,400
So it depends on the situation.

749
00:32:33,400 --> 00:32:36,800
Exactly. If it's your first email, be more formal.

750
00:32:36,800 --> 00:32:40,000
Make sense. Now, what comes after the greeting?

751
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:41,600
The opening sentence.

752
00:32:41,600 --> 00:32:43,200
This sets the tone, right?

753
00:32:43,200 --> 00:32:49,000
Yes. You can say, I hope you are doing well, or I hope this email finds you well.

754
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:52,500
Or be direct, like I am writing to request.

755
00:32:52,500 --> 00:32:55,100
Exactly. Keep it polite and clear.

756
00:32:55,100 --> 00:32:57,140
Avoid being too casual.

757
00:32:57,140 --> 00:32:58,260
Definitely.

758
00:32:58,260 --> 00:33:01,180
Now let's talk about the body of the email.

759
00:33:01,180 --> 00:33:02,940
This is the main message.

760
00:33:02,940 --> 00:33:06,220
Yes, keep it organized with short paragraphs.

761
00:33:06,220 --> 00:33:08,100
One idea per paragraph.

762
00:33:08,100 --> 00:33:08,980
Exactly.

763
00:33:08,980 --> 00:33:11,180
And avoid long sentences.

764
00:33:11,180 --> 00:33:12,500
People are busy.

765
00:33:12,500 --> 00:33:15,340
Very busy, so clarity is key.

766
00:33:15,340 --> 00:33:16,900
And we should be polite.

767
00:33:16,900 --> 00:33:19,780
Yes, use phrases like, could you please

768
00:33:19,780 --> 00:33:22,180
or I would appreciate it if.

769
00:33:22,180 --> 00:33:24,660
That sounds much better than giving orders.

770
00:33:24,660 --> 00:33:25,660
Exactly.

771
00:33:25,660 --> 00:33:27,840
Now how do we close the email?

772
00:33:27,840 --> 00:33:33,540
Use polite phrases like thank you for your time or I look forward to your response.

773
00:33:33,540 --> 00:33:34,540
And then?

774
00:33:34,540 --> 00:33:38,580
Add a closing like best regards or kind regards.

775
00:33:38,580 --> 00:33:39,580
And your name.

776
00:33:39,580 --> 00:33:40,580
Correct.

777
00:33:40,580 --> 00:33:42,460
Let's do a full example.

778
00:33:42,460 --> 00:33:43,460
Great idea.

779
00:33:43,460 --> 00:33:45,740
Subject meeting request.

780
00:33:45,740 --> 00:33:47,140
Dear Mr. Brown.

781
00:33:47,140 --> 00:33:48,660
I hope you are doing well.

782
00:33:48,660 --> 00:33:52,980
I am writing to request a meeting to discuss the new project.

783
00:33:52,980 --> 00:33:54,540
Are you available this week?

784
00:33:54,540 --> 00:33:58,140
I am available on Wednesday or Thursday afternoon.

785
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:00,220
Please let me know your availability.

786
00:34:00,220 --> 00:34:01,780
Thank you for your time.

787
00:34:01,780 --> 00:34:03,660
Best regards, Jack.

788
00:34:03,660 --> 00:34:05,580
That's simple and professional.

789
00:34:05,580 --> 00:34:08,300
Yes, now let's talk about mistakes.

790
00:34:08,300 --> 00:34:11,060
First mistake, being too informal.

791
00:34:11,060 --> 00:34:13,260
Like, hey bro, or what's up?

792
00:34:13,260 --> 00:34:15,260
Exactly, not appropriate.

793
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:17,500
Second mistake, grammar errors.

794
00:34:17,500 --> 00:34:19,020
Always proofread.

795
00:34:19,020 --> 00:34:21,580
Third mistake, writing too much.

796
00:34:21,580 --> 00:34:22,660
Keep it short.

797
00:34:22,660 --> 00:34:25,140
Fourth mistake, being too direct.

798
00:34:25,140 --> 00:34:26,980
Use polite language.

799
00:34:26,980 --> 00:34:28,740
Now let's do a role play.

800
00:34:28,740 --> 00:34:29,580
Okay.

801
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:30,780
Dear Speaker One.

802
00:34:30,780 --> 00:34:32,780
Hello, how can I help you?

803
00:34:32,780 --> 00:34:35,740
I am writing to inform you that I will be late tomorrow.

804
00:34:35,740 --> 00:34:37,460
Thank you for letting me know.

805
00:34:37,460 --> 00:34:39,660
I will arrive around 11 a.m.

806
00:34:39,660 --> 00:34:40,660
That's fine.

807
00:34:40,660 --> 00:34:42,380
Thank you for your understanding.

808
00:34:42,380 --> 00:34:43,380
No problem.

809
00:34:43,380 --> 00:34:45,460
Best regards, Speaker One.

810
00:34:45,460 --> 00:34:48,020
Great, now a client email.

811
00:34:48,020 --> 00:34:49,340
Dear Miss Taylor.

812
00:34:49,340 --> 00:34:50,980
Thank you for your message.

813
00:34:50,980 --> 00:34:52,860
We have received your request.

814
00:34:52,860 --> 00:34:55,660
We will respond within two business days.

815
00:34:55,660 --> 00:34:58,180
Please contact us if you need anything else.

816
00:34:58,180 --> 00:35:00,380
Kind regards, speaker one.

817
00:35:00,380 --> 00:35:01,740
Very professional.

818
00:35:01,740 --> 00:35:04,140
Now, let's talk about tone.

819
00:35:04,140 --> 00:35:06,460
Tone is how your message feels.

820
00:35:06,460 --> 00:35:09,260
Yes, polite versus rude.

821
00:35:09,260 --> 00:35:13,020
Send me the file versus could you please send me the file?

822
00:35:13,020 --> 00:35:14,220
Big difference.

823
00:35:14,220 --> 00:35:15,700
Now replies.

824
00:35:15,700 --> 00:35:17,260
Always acknowledge.

825
00:35:17,260 --> 00:35:18,780
Thank you for your email.

826
00:35:18,780 --> 00:35:20,740
Then respond clearly.

827
00:35:20,740 --> 00:35:22,100
And stay polite.

828
00:35:22,100 --> 00:35:23,100
Always.

829
00:35:23,100 --> 00:35:24,540
Now complaints.

830
00:35:24,540 --> 00:35:26,420
Be polite but firm.

831
00:35:26,420 --> 00:35:28,900
I am writing to express my concern.

832
00:35:28,900 --> 00:35:30,020
Good start.

833
00:35:30,020 --> 00:35:31,980
The product was damaged.

834
00:35:31,980 --> 00:35:32,820
Clear.

835
00:35:32,820 --> 00:35:34,540
I would appreciate your help.

836
00:35:34,540 --> 00:35:35,500
Professional.

837
00:35:35,500 --> 00:35:37,300
Now follow up emails.

838
00:35:37,300 --> 00:35:38,820
Very important.

839
00:35:38,820 --> 00:35:41,860
I just wanted to follow up on my previous email.

840
00:35:41,860 --> 00:35:44,100
Please let me know your availability.

841
00:35:44,100 --> 00:35:45,380
Thank you again.

842
00:35:45,380 --> 00:35:47,620
Best regards, speaker one.

843
00:35:47,620 --> 00:35:48,700
Perfect.

844
00:35:48,700 --> 00:35:50,380
Now let's summarize.

845
00:35:50,380 --> 00:35:57,260
clear subject line, clear greeting, polite opening, clear body, professional closing,

846
00:35:57,260 --> 00:36:04,700
check grammar and tone. Exactly. Now a question for you. What emails do you write most often?

847
00:36:04,700 --> 00:36:11,020
Practice writing them in English. Practice is key. The more you write, the better you get.

848
00:36:11,020 --> 00:36:15,980
Thank you for joining us today. Keep practicing and improving. See you next time.

849
00:36:15,980 --> 00:36:21,740
Goodbye! Hello! Welcome back Business English Learners! We're really happy to have you with us today.

850
00:36:21,740 --> 00:36:27,020
Hi everyone! Thanks for joining us. If you're here to improve your English, especially your

851
00:36:27,020 --> 00:36:32,700
professional communication skills, you're in the right place. I'm Sarah. And I'm Daniel. And

852
00:36:32,700 --> 00:36:38,860
today's episode is going to be extremely practical and useful for anyone working or planning to

853
00:36:38,860 --> 00:36:43,820
work in a professional environment. That's right! Today we're talking about a skill that can

854
00:36:43,820 --> 00:36:46,900
completely change how people see you at work.

855
00:36:46,900 --> 00:36:47,900
Yes.

856
00:36:47,900 --> 00:36:52,420
How to write an email professionally in business English.

857
00:36:52,420 --> 00:36:53,700
Emails are everywhere.

858
00:36:53,700 --> 00:36:58,500
At work, in business, in studies, and even when applying for jobs.

859
00:36:58,500 --> 00:37:03,820
And the truth is, a well-written email can open doors while a poorly written one can

860
00:37:03,820 --> 00:37:07,420
create confusion or even damage your reputation.

861
00:37:07,420 --> 00:37:08,420
Exactly.

862
00:37:08,420 --> 00:37:09,420
But don't worry.

863
00:37:09,420 --> 00:37:13,300
Today, we'll guide you step by step in a very simple and clear way.

864
00:37:13,300 --> 00:37:19,980
We'll give you practical examples, useful phrases, real-life situations, and even role-plays.

865
00:37:19,980 --> 00:37:26,200
So Daniel, let me ask you, how important do you think email writing is in business communication?

866
00:37:26,200 --> 00:37:27,780
I think it's essential.

867
00:37:27,780 --> 00:37:31,580
In many cases, your email is the first impression you make.

868
00:37:31,580 --> 00:37:34,740
People judge your professionalism based on how you write.

869
00:37:34,740 --> 00:37:35,940
That's so true.

870
00:37:35,940 --> 00:37:40,520
Even if your English isn't perfect, a clear and polite email can still make a great

871
00:37:40,520 --> 00:37:41,520
impression.

872
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:42,520
Absolutely.

873
00:37:42,520 --> 00:37:46,640
Let's start from the very beginning, the structure of a professional email.

874
00:37:46,640 --> 00:37:52,160
Yes, every professional email has a structure, and once you understand it, everything becomes

875
00:37:52,160 --> 00:37:53,160
easier.

876
00:37:53,160 --> 00:37:54,960
The first part is the greeting.

877
00:37:54,960 --> 00:37:58,040
Right, the greeting is how you start your email.

878
00:37:58,040 --> 00:37:59,400
It sets the tone.

879
00:37:59,400 --> 00:38:02,320
So what are some professional greetings we can use?

880
00:38:02,320 --> 00:38:07,240
The most common ones are Dear Name and Hello Name.

881
00:38:07,240 --> 00:38:10,320
Which one should we use in formal situations?

882
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:15,680
is more formal. It's perfect for managers, clients, or people you don't know well.

883
00:38:15,680 --> 00:38:16,680
And hello?

884
00:38:16,680 --> 00:38:21,200
Hello is slightly more relaxed, but still professional. It's great for colleagues.

885
00:38:21,200 --> 00:38:22,520
What about high?

886
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:26,920
High is acceptable in many workplaces, but it depends on the company culture.

887
00:38:26,920 --> 00:38:32,760
Okay, so if I'm writing to my boss, I should probably say, Dear Mr. Smith.

888
00:38:32,760 --> 00:38:35,440
Exactly. That's safe and professional.

889
00:38:35,440 --> 00:38:37,880
What if I don't know the person's name?

890
00:38:37,880 --> 00:38:42,620
You can say Dear Sir or Madam or Dear Hiring Manager.

891
00:38:42,620 --> 00:38:44,740
That sounds formal but appropriate.

892
00:38:44,740 --> 00:38:48,620
Yes, always better to be slightly formal than too casual.

893
00:38:48,620 --> 00:38:51,960
Alright, after the greeting, what comes next?

894
00:38:51,960 --> 00:38:53,300
The opening line.

895
00:38:53,300 --> 00:38:55,600
This is where you introduce your purpose.

896
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:58,420
So this is where we say why we are writing.

897
00:38:58,420 --> 00:38:59,420
Exactly.

898
00:38:59,420 --> 00:39:02,240
A very common phrase is I am writing to.

899
00:39:02,240 --> 00:39:03,600
Can you give examples?

900
00:39:03,600 --> 00:39:04,600
Sure.

901
00:39:04,600 --> 00:39:06,960
I am writing to request information.

902
00:39:06,960 --> 00:39:09,720
Or I am writing to follow up on our meeting.

903
00:39:09,720 --> 00:39:10,720
Perfect.

904
00:39:10,720 --> 00:39:11,960
That's clear and direct.

905
00:39:11,960 --> 00:39:14,320
What if we want to sound a bit more friendly?

906
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:16,920
You can start with, I hope you are doing well.

907
00:39:16,920 --> 00:39:18,800
Or I hope you had a great day.

908
00:39:18,800 --> 00:39:20,160
Yes, but keep it short.

909
00:39:20,160 --> 00:39:23,480
Don't write long, personal messages in business emails.

910
00:39:23,480 --> 00:39:24,480
Got it.

911
00:39:24,480 --> 00:39:25,880
Short and professional.

912
00:39:25,880 --> 00:39:28,680
Now let's move to the main body of the email.

913
00:39:28,680 --> 00:39:31,560
This is where we explain everything in detail, right?

914
00:39:31,560 --> 00:39:34,480
Yes, but remember, clarity is key.

915
00:39:34,480 --> 00:39:36,640
What do you mean by clarity?

916
00:39:36,640 --> 00:39:38,200
Use simple language.

917
00:39:38,200 --> 00:39:40,640
Avoid long, complicated sentences.

918
00:39:40,640 --> 00:39:46,440
So instead of saying something long and confusing, say something short and direct.

919
00:39:46,440 --> 00:39:47,960
Can you give an example?

920
00:39:47,960 --> 00:39:51,560
Instead of saying, I was wondering if it would be possible for you to send me the

921
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:52,720
document.

922
00:39:52,720 --> 00:39:54,040
That's very long.

923
00:39:54,040 --> 00:39:57,720
Yes, you can say, could you please send me the document?

924
00:39:57,720 --> 00:39:58,720
Much better.

925
00:39:58,720 --> 00:40:01,600
Now let's talk about making requests politely.

926
00:40:01,600 --> 00:40:03,640
This is very important in business English.

927
00:40:03,640 --> 00:40:10,760
Yes, you can use phrases like could you please or would you mind or I would appreciate it if you...

928
00:40:10,760 --> 00:40:14,200
For example, could you please confirm the meeting time?

929
00:40:14,200 --> 00:40:18,120
Exactly. Or I would appreciate it if you could send the report.

930
00:40:18,120 --> 00:40:19,880
That sounds very polite.

931
00:40:19,880 --> 00:40:22,200
Now let's talk about giving information.

932
00:40:22,200 --> 00:40:22,840
Okay.

933
00:40:22,840 --> 00:40:27,880
You can say please note that or I would like to inform you that.

934
00:40:27,880 --> 00:40:31,720
For example, please note that the deadline has changed.

935
00:40:31,720 --> 00:40:33,080
That's very clear.

936
00:40:33,080 --> 00:40:35,580
Now, let's talk about tone.

937
00:40:35,580 --> 00:40:39,000
Tone is very important, especially in emails.

938
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:41,640
Yes, because people cannot hear your voice.

939
00:40:41,640 --> 00:40:43,520
They only read your words.

940
00:40:43,520 --> 00:40:47,040
So if you write something too direct, it can sound rude.

941
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:47,920
Exactly.

942
00:40:47,920 --> 00:40:50,320
For example, send me the file.

943
00:40:50,320 --> 00:40:51,920
That sounds like a command.

944
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:52,680
Yes.

945
00:40:52,680 --> 00:40:55,720
Better to say, could you please send me the file?

946
00:40:55,720 --> 00:40:57,040
Much more polite.

947
00:40:57,040 --> 00:40:59,160
Now, what if we need to complain?

948
00:40:59,160 --> 00:41:01,040
That's a difficult situation.

949
00:41:01,040 --> 00:41:03,560
Yes, but you should always stay polite.

950
00:41:03,560 --> 00:41:06,640
So instead of saying, you made a mistake,

951
00:41:06,640 --> 00:41:09,800
you can say, it seems there may be an issue.

952
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:11,240
That sounds softer.

953
00:41:11,240 --> 00:41:12,120
Exactly.

954
00:41:12,120 --> 00:41:14,840
Now let's move to the closing part of the email.

955
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:17,080
How do we finish professionally?

956
00:41:17,080 --> 00:41:19,120
First, add a closing sentence

957
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:21,560
like looking forward to your response.

958
00:41:21,560 --> 00:41:23,520
Or thank you for your time.

959
00:41:23,520 --> 00:41:25,600
Yes, then add a sign-off.

960
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:27,400
Like best regards.

961
00:41:27,400 --> 00:41:29,280
Or kind regards.

962
00:41:29,280 --> 00:41:30,640
Or sincerely.

963
00:41:30,640 --> 00:41:31,480
Exactly.

964
00:41:31,480 --> 00:41:33,480
Which one is the most common?

965
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:36,560
Best regards is the safest and most common.

966
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:40,300
All right, now let's create a full email together.

967
00:41:40,300 --> 00:41:41,240
Great idea.

968
00:41:41,240 --> 00:41:44,120
Dear Sarah, I hope you are doing well.

969
00:41:44,120 --> 00:41:47,040
I am writing to schedule a meeting this week.

970
00:41:47,040 --> 00:41:49,400
Could you please let me know your availability?

971
00:41:49,400 --> 00:41:53,040
Looking forward to your response, best regards, Daniel.

972
00:41:53,040 --> 00:41:56,000
That's perfect, clear, polite, and professional.

973
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:56,840
Thank you.

974
00:41:56,840 --> 00:41:59,160
Now let's talk about common mistakes.

975
00:41:59,160 --> 00:42:01,160
Yes, this is very important.

976
00:42:01,160 --> 00:42:04,160
Mistake number one being too informal.

977
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:07,160
Like writing, hey, or what's up.

978
00:42:07,160 --> 00:42:10,160
Exactly, avoid slang in business emails.

979
00:42:10,160 --> 00:42:13,160
Mistake number two, no greeting.

980
00:42:13,160 --> 00:42:15,160
Always start with a greeting.

981
00:42:15,160 --> 00:42:18,160
Mistake number three, long paragraphs.

982
00:42:18,160 --> 00:42:20,160
Keep your email easy to read.

983
00:42:20,160 --> 00:42:22,160
Mistake number four, grammar mistakes.

984
00:42:22,160 --> 00:42:27,160
Yes, like I send you yesterday instead of I sent you yesterday.

985
00:42:27,160 --> 00:42:30,600
Mistake number five, unclear subject line.

986
00:42:30,600 --> 00:42:33,880
Always write a clear subject like meeting request.

987
00:42:33,880 --> 00:42:35,640
Now let's do a role play.

988
00:42:35,640 --> 00:42:38,680
Great. Let's imagine you need to write to your manager.

989
00:42:38,680 --> 00:42:41,960
Okay. Hi. I can't finish the report.

990
00:42:41,960 --> 00:42:43,640
That sounds too direct.

991
00:42:43,640 --> 00:42:45,080
How can I improve it?

992
00:42:45,080 --> 00:42:46,280
Try this.

993
00:42:46,280 --> 00:42:48,680
Dear Sarah, I hope you are doing well.

994
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:53,080
I wanted to inform you that there may be a delay in completing the report.

995
00:42:53,080 --> 00:42:55,320
I apologize for any inconvenience.

996
00:42:55,320 --> 00:42:56,920
Best regards, Daniel.

997
00:42:56,920 --> 00:42:58,360
That sounds much better.

998
00:42:58,360 --> 00:43:01,080
Yes, always be polite and professional.

999
00:43:01,080 --> 00:43:02,520
Let's do another example.

1000
00:43:02,520 --> 00:43:04,120
Email to a client.

1001
00:43:04,120 --> 00:43:06,200
Okay, send payment.

1002
00:43:06,200 --> 00:43:07,720
That's too rude.

1003
00:43:07,720 --> 00:43:09,080
Yes, definitely.

1004
00:43:09,080 --> 00:43:10,200
Better to say,

1005
00:43:10,200 --> 00:43:11,400
Dear Mr. Brown,

1006
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:12,920
I hope you are doing well.

1007
00:43:12,920 --> 00:43:15,800
This is a friendly reminder regarding your payment.

1008
00:43:15,800 --> 00:43:18,280
Please let us know if you need any assistance.

1009
00:43:18,280 --> 00:43:19,400
Best regards.

1010
00:43:19,400 --> 00:43:21,320
That sounds much more professional.

1011
00:43:21,320 --> 00:43:24,120
Exactly, tone makes a big difference.

1012
00:43:24,120 --> 00:43:26,440
I feel like I understand email writing

1013
00:43:26,440 --> 00:43:32,760
much better now. That's great. And remember, practice is key. Yes, try writing emails every

1014
00:43:32,760 --> 00:43:38,120
day, even short ones. And always check your grammar and tone before sending. All right,

1015
00:43:38,120 --> 00:43:43,080
everyone, let's wrap up. Today we learned how to write professional emails in business

1016
00:43:43,080 --> 00:43:48,520
English. We covered greetings, structure, useful phrases and common mistakes. And

1017
00:43:48,520 --> 00:43:54,240
we practiced real examples. The most important thing is to keep your emails clear, polite

1018
00:43:54,240 --> 00:43:55,320
and professional.

1019
00:43:55,320 --> 00:43:56,240
Exactly.

1020
00:43:56,240 --> 00:43:57,960
Thank you so much for listening.

1021
00:43:57,960 --> 00:44:00,320
Keep practicing and improving your English.

1022
00:44:00,320 --> 00:44:02,120
We'll see you in the next episode.

1023
00:44:02,120 --> 00:44:03,160
Goodbye, everyone.
